/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:6, name:"The Prophet's Vision and Call", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 6:8",verse:"Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? Then said I, Here am I; send me."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah's vision came in the year King Uzziah died, around 740 BC? Uzziah had reigned about 52 years, so most people in Judah had never known another king, yet Isaiah saw the King who never dies.","Did you know 'seraphim' means 'burning ones'? They are named only here in the whole Bible, and though each had six wings, they used four of them just to cover themselves before God.","Did you know Isaiah's commission was one of the hardest ever given? He was sent to preach to a people who would not listen, and Jesus later quoted this chapter to explain why some hearts stay closed."], questions:[
{q:"In the year of which king's death did Isaiah see the Lord sitting upon a throne?", options:["King Uzziah","King Ahaz","King Hezekiah","King David"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:1",verse:"In the year that king Uzziah died I saw also the Lord sitting upon a throne, high and lifted up, and his train filled the temple.", insight:"Visions often arrive when an era ends. When the longest-reigning king in Judah's memory died, Isaiah saw the King who never dies, and earthly loss became the doorway to heavenly sight."},
{q:"What filled the temple when Isaiah saw the Lord, high and lifted up?", options:["His train","A bright fire","A rushing wind","Thick darkness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:1",verse:"In the year that king Uzziah died I saw also the Lord sitting upon a throne, high and lifted up, and his train filled the temple.", insight:"The train of His robe filled the temple, majesty overflowing its borders. Worship begins not with our requests but with beholding a glory too large for the house built to hold it."},
{q:"How many wings did each of the seraphims have?", options:["Six","Four","Two","Ten"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:2",verse:"Above it stood the seraphims: each one had six wings; with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly.", insight:"Even the highest created beings are richly furnished, yet their glory is all spent in reverence. Nearness to God increases humility, not pride."},
{q:"With two of their six wings, what did the seraphims do?", options:["They covered their faces","They beat the air like drums","They shaded the throne","They sheltered Isaiah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:2",verse:"Above it stood the seraphims: each one had six wings; with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly.", insight:"They veil their faces with two wings and their feet with two, using only two to fly. Holiness hides itself before greater holiness, and true worship covers what it could display."},
{q:"What did one seraphim cry to another in the vision?", options:["Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts","Peace, peace, peace, to all the earth","Glory, glory, glory, forevermore","Hosanna, hosanna, in the highest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:3",verse:"And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory.", insight:"Holiness is heaven's main theme, repeated three times for emphasis. Before God ever speaks of love or judgment, His angels declare what He is in Himself."},
{q:"According to the seraphims' cry, what is the whole earth full of?", options:["His glory","His judgment","His thunder","His mystery"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:3",verse:"And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory.", insight:"Glory is not locked inside the temple. The whole earth is saturated with it, which means every field and street is holy ground waiting to be noticed."},
{q:"What moved at the voice of him that cried in Isaiah's vision?", options:["The posts of the door","The stones of the altar","The curtains of the temple","The pillars of the ark"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:4",verse:"And the posts of the door moved at the voice of him that cried, and the house was filled with smoke.", insight:"The doorposts shook and the house filled with smoke. God's voice moves foundations, and worship that leaves us entirely unmoved has not truly heard Him."},
{q:"What did Isaiah cry out when he saw the King, the LORD of hosts?", options:["Woe is me! for I am undone","Blessed am I, for I am chosen","Hide me in the cleft of the rock","Let me sing His praise forever"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:5",verse:"Then said I, Woe is me! for I am undone; because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people of unclean lips: for mine eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts.", insight:"Seeing God's holiness made Isaiah see himself truly. The first honest response to a clear vision of God is never self-congratulation but confession."},
{q:"What did Isaiah call himself after seeing the Lord?", options:["A man of unclean lips","A prophet without honor","A child in understanding","A stranger to the covenant"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:5",verse:"Then said I, Woe is me! for I am undone; because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people of unclean lips: for mine eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts.", insight:"He names his exact sin, unclean lips, the very tools of his calling. Repentance gets specific, and the prophet's mouth had to be cleansed before it could carry God's word."},
{q:"What did one of the seraphims bring to Isaiah?", options:["A live coal from off the altar","A golden crown","A scroll of the law","A cup of water"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:6",verse:"Then flew one of the seraphims unto me, having a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar:", insight:"Cleansing came from God's altar, not from Isaiah's efforts. Heaven supplies what holiness demands, and grace always arrives from God's side first."},
{q:"How had the seraphim taken the live coal from the altar?", options:["With the tongs","With his bare hands","With the hem of his robe","With a golden censer"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:6",verse:"Then flew one of the seraphims unto me, having a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar:", insight:"Even the cleansing fire is handled with reverence. God's mercy is holy, never casual, and the instruments of grace are treated as sacred things."},
{q:"When the coal touched Isaiah's lips, what did the seraphim say had happened?", options:["Thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin purged","Thy voice shall be as thunder","Thou art sealed for the ministry","Thy words shall never fail"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:7",verse:"And he laid it upon my mouth, and said, Lo, this hath touched thy lips; and thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin purged.", insight:"God speaks forgiveness before He gives a mission. The called must first be cleansed, because no one can carry God's word with a guilty conscience."},
{q:"What question did Isaiah hear from the voice of the Lord?", options:["Whom shall I send, and who will go for us?","Who will stand in the gap for Judah?","Who is worthy to open the scroll?","Who will speak to the kings of the earth?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:8",verse:"Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? Then said I, Here am I; send me.", insight:"God invites rather than conscripts. The mission of heaven begins with a question, leaving room for a willing heart to answer."},
{q:"How did Isaiah answer God's call?", options:["Here am I; send me","Lord, send someone else","I am too young to go","Let me first count the cost"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:8",verse:"Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? Then said I, Here am I; send me.", insight:"Cleansed lips became a willing voice. The truest answer to God's question was not a resume but availability, spoken before the assignment was even described."},
{q:"What hard message was Isaiah told to speak to the people?", options:["Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not","Return to me, and I will return to you","Repent, for the kingdom is at hand","Peace, peace, when there is no peace"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:9",verse:"And he said, Go, and tell this people, Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not.", insight:"Faithful preaching does not guarantee a listening audience. The prophet's duty is to speak God's word, not to manufacture results, and success is measured by obedience."},
{q:"What would happen to the heart of the people, according to God's word?", options:["It would be made fat","It would be turned to stone","It would melt like wax","It would be cut in two"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:10",verse:"Make the heart of this people fat, and make their ears heavy, and shut their eyes; lest they see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and convert, and be healed.", insight:"Persistent refusal hardens the heart, and God sometimes confirms the direction we keep choosing. This is the sober warning that today's hardness becomes tomorrow's inability."},
{q:"When Isaiah asked 'Lord, how long?', what was the answer?", options:["Until the cities be wasted without inhabitant","Until the king repents in sackcloth","Until the captives return from exile","Until the temple falls to the ground"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:11",verse:"Then said I, Lord, how long? And he answered, Until the cities be wasted without inhabitant, and the houses without man, and the land be utterly desolate,", insight:"Ministry is measured by faithfulness, not outcomes. Isaiah was called to preach through desolation, which teaches that God's servants do not get to choose their results."},
{q:"What would the LORD do before the remnant remained in the land?", options:["Remove men far away","Destroy the temple utterly","Scatter the priests abroad","Silence every prophet"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:12",verse:"And the LORD have removed men far away, and there be a great forsaking in the midst of the land.", insight:"There would be a great forsaking in the midst of the land. God empties before He refills, and even the emptiness is mercy making room for a new beginning."},
{q:"After the judgment, what portion would remain in the land?", options:["A tenth","A single household","The temple only","Nothing at all"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:13",verse:"But yet in it shall be a tenth, and it shall return, and shall be eaten: as a teil tree, and as an oak, whose substance is in them, when they cast their leaves: so the holy seed shall be the substance thereof.", insight:"Judgment never wipes out God's people entirely. A tenth remains, because wrath always carries a remnant of grace inside it."},
{q:"To what trees is the surviving remnant compared at the end of the chapter?", options:["A teil tree and an oak","A cedar and a palm","A fig tree and a vine","A cypress and an olive"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 6:13",verse:"But yet in it shall be a tenth, and it shall return, and shall be eaten: as a teil tree, and as an oak, whose substance is in them, when they cast their leaves: so the holy seed shall be the substance thereof.", insight:"Like trees cut down to the stump, the remnant keeps its root, for the holy seed is the substance thereof. God's future grows from a small, faithful root that judgment cannot kill."}
]},
{n:7, name:"The Sign of Immanuel", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 7:14",verse:"Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 7:14 is one of the most famous prophecies in Scripture? About 700 years after Isaiah spoke it, Matthew quoted 'a virgin shall conceive... Immanuel' to describe the birth of Jesus.","Did you know Ahaz met Isaiah at the conduit of the upper pool, the very water source King Hezekiah later tapped with his famous tunnel? That tunnel still exists under Jerusalem today.","Did you know both enemy kings fell just as Isaiah said? Assyria killed Rezin of Syria and took Damascus, while Pekah of Israel was assassinated by one of his own officers."], questions:[
{q:"In the days of which king of Judah did Syria and Israel march against Jerusalem?", options:["Ahaz","Hezekiah","Jotham","Manasseh"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the days of Ahaz the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, king of Judah, that Rezin the king of Syria, and Pekah the son of Remaliah, king of Israel, went up toward Jerusalem to war against it, but could not prevail against it.", insight:"Trouble visits every reign, good and bad alike. The question is never whether pressure will come, but where the heart turns when it does."},
{q:"Which two kings formed an alliance against Jerusalem?", options:["Rezin of Syria and Pekah of Israel","Tiglath of Assyria and Hoshea","Pharaoh of Egypt and Hazael","Benhadad and Elah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the days of Ahaz the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, king of Judah, that Rezin the king of Syria, and Pekah the son of Remaliah, king of Israel, went up toward Jerusalem to war against it, but could not prevail against it.", insight:"Enemies unite faster than friends. Israel joined a foreign power against its own brother nation, showing how fear makes strange and tragic alliances."},
{q:"To what were the shaken hearts of Ahaz and his people compared?", options:["Trees of the wood moved with the wind","Leaves caught in a whirlwind","Waves tossed by the sea","Reeds bent by a flood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:2",verse:"And it was told the house of David, saying, Syria is confederate with Ephraim. And his heart was moved, and the heart of his people, as the trees of the wood are moved with the wind.", insight:"Hearts without roots shake at every wind. Fear was the symptom, but faithlessness was the cause, and a moved heart reveals an unanchored trust."},
{q:"Which of Isaiah's sons went with him to meet King Ahaz?", options:["Shear-jashub","Maher-shalal-hash-baz","Immanuel","Jezreel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:3",verse:"Then said the LORD unto Isaiah, Go forth now to meet Ahaz, thou, and Shear-jashub thy son, at the end of the conduit of the upper pool in the highway of the fuller’s field;", insight:"The boy's name meant 'a remnant shall return.' Isaiah carried hope with him as a living sign, so that the king saw the promise walking beside the prophet."},
{q:"Where did Isaiah meet King Ahaz?", options:["At the end of the conduit of the upper pool","At the gate of the temple","In the throne room of the palace","On the field of battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:3",verse:"Then said the LORD unto Isaiah, Go forth now to meet Ahaz, thou, and Shear-jashub thy son, at the end of the conduit of the upper pool in the highway of the fuller’s field;", insight:"God met the king at the waterworks, not the throne, where Ahaz was likely inspecting defenses instead of seeking God. We often prepare everywhere except in prayer."},
{q:"What was God's first word to the frightened king?", options:["Take heed, and be quiet; fear not","Gather your army and fight","Send tribute to Assyria","Flee to Egypt for help"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:4",verse:"And say unto him, Take heed, and be quiet; fear not, neither be fainthearted for the two tails of these smoking firebrands, for the fierce anger of Rezin with Syria, and of the son of Remaliah.", insight:"God's first word to fear is 'be quiet.' Stillness is often the hardest form of trust, because it feels like doing nothing while God does everything."},
{q:"What did God call the two threatening kings?", options:["Two tails of smoking firebrands","Two roaring lions","Two broken bows","Two withered branches"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:4",verse:"And say unto him, Take heed, and be quiet; fear not, neither be fainthearted for the two tails of these smoking firebrands, for the fierce anger of Rezin with Syria, and of the son of Remaliah.", insight:"Heaven sees threats smaller than we do. To God, two kings were dying embers, not blazing fires, which puts our own terrors in their true proportion."},
{q:"What was the name of the man the enemies wanted to set as king over Judah?", options:["The son of Tabeal","The son of Remaliah","The son of Jeberechiah","The son of Uzziah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:6",verse:"Let us go up against Judah, and vex it, and let us make a breach therein for us, and set a king in the midst of it, even the son of Tabeal:", insight:"Conspirators always have a replacement king in mind. But the throne of David was God's to guard, and no plot could install whom heaven had not chosen."},
{q:"What did the Lord GOD say about the enemies' plot?", options:["It shall not stand, neither shall it come to pass","It shall prosper for a season","It shall be delayed but not stopped","It shall slowly turn against them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:7",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD, It shall not stand, neither shall it come to pass.", insight:"Some plans never reach the field. God can end a conspiracy with a single sentence, and His 'it shall not stand' is stronger than any army's 'we will.'"},
{q:"Within how many years would Ephraim be broken, so as to be not a people?", options:["Threescore and five years","Seven years","Forty years","Seventy years"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:8",verse:"For the head of Syria is Damascus, and the head of Damascus is Rezin; and within threescore and five years shall Ephraim be broken, that it be not a people.", insight:"God sets dates on empires. Nations rise and fall on His timetable, not on their own pride, and history keeps His appointments."},
{q:"What sign was Ahaz invited to ask of the LORD his God?", options:["Any sign, either in the depth or in the height above","A sign confirmed by the temple priests","A sign given only from heaven","A sign written in the book of the law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:11",verse:"Ask thee a sign of the LORD thy God; ask it either in the depth, or in the height above.", insight:"God offered a blank check of assurance, any sign, anywhere. The generosity of the offer exposes how small our asking usually is."},
{q:"How did Ahaz answer the offer of a sign?", options:["I will not ask, neither will I tempt the LORD","Give me a sign from the deep","Let the prophet choose the sign","I need time to consider it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:12",verse:"But Ahaz said, I will not ask, neither will I tempt the LORD.", insight:"Piety can mask unbelief. Ahaz sounded reverent while refusing God's gift, proving that religious language is no proof of a trusting heart."},
{q:"Since Ahaz would not ask, what sign would the Lord Himself give?", options:["A virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel","The sun would stand still at noon","Fire would fall from heaven","The temple veil would be torn"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:14",verse:"Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.", insight:"When man refuses to ask, God gives anyway. The greatest sign in history came to people who would not seek it, which is the very definition of grace."},
{q:"What name would the promised child be called?", options:["Immanuel","Shear-jashub","Maher-shalal-hash-baz","Pekah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:14",verse:"Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.", insight:"God chose the name Himself. Immanuel is heaven's own pledge that God would not stay distant but would come to dwell with His people."},
{q:"What would the child eat as he grew up?", options:["Butter and honey","Bread and water","Milk and dates","Meat and wine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:15",verse:"Butter and honey shall he eat, that he may know to refuse the evil, and choose the good.", insight:"Butter and honey signal a land reduced to pasture. Judgment simplifies life to its basics, and even the promised child would grow up in lean times."},
{q:"Before the child knew good from evil, what would happen to the two dreaded kings?", options:["Their land would be forsaken of both her kings","The temple would be rebuilt","Assyria would fall to Babylon","Judah would be exalted over all"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:16",verse:"For before the child shall know to refuse the evil, and choose the good, the land that thou abhorrest shall be forsaken of both her kings.", insight:"God's clock ran on a child's childhood. The great threat had an expiry date only a few years away, but Ahaz could not wait on God's timing."},
{q:"What worse trouble did the LORD promise to bring upon Ahaz instead?", options:["The king of Assyria","The armies of Egypt","A famine of seven years","A plague upon the land"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:17",verse:"The LORD shall bring upon thee, and upon thy people, and upon thy father’s house, days that have not come, from the day that Ephraim departed from Judah; even the king of Assyria.", insight:"The rescue Ahaz refused became the judgment he endured. The ally he hired became his conqueror, a warning that our chosen saviors often become our masters."},
{q:"What vivid picture describes the coming humiliation by Assyria?", options:["The Lord would shave with a hired razor","The Lord would shut the gates of heaven","The Lord would dry up the rivers","The Lord would break every bow"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:20",verse:"In the same day shall the Lord shave with a razor that is hired, namely, by them beyond the river, by the king of Assyria, the head, and the hair of the feet: and it shall also consume the beard.", insight:"Being shaved meant total disgrace and loss. God uses even pagan kings as His instruments, and a 'hired razor' cuts exactly where He directs it."},
{q:"In that day, what would every place of a thousand vines become?", options:["Briers and thorns","A fruitful field","A pasture for sheep","A heap of stones"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:23",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that every place shall be, where there were a thousand vines at a thousand silverlings, it shall even be for briers and thorns.", insight:"Vineyards speak of settled prosperity. When God withdraws His hand, cultivation returns to wilderness, and the land forgets what human hands once made of it."},
{q:"What would men bring with them when they came into the ruined land?", options:["Arrows and bows","Plows and seed","Oxen and carts","Gold and silver"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 7:24",verse:"With arrows and with bows shall men come thither; because all the land shall become briers and thorns.", insight:"The land of harvest becomes a hunting ground. Judgment reverses a nation's fortunes, turning farmland into a place fit only for archers."}
]},
{n:8, name:"Swift to the Spoil", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 8:13",verse:"Sanctify the LORD of hosts himself; and let him be your fear, and let him be your dread."}, facts:["Did you know Maher-shalal-hash-baz is the longest personal name in the Bible? It means 'swift to the spoil, hasty to the prey,' a walking headline of Assyria's coming invasion.","Did you know the 'waters of Shiloah' were the gentle stream that fed Jerusalem's pool of Siloam? The people despised God's quiet provision and chose the loud, violent 'river' of Assyria instead.","Did you know Isaiah 8:14 is quoted in the New Testament about Jesus? Both Romans and 1 Peter call Him the 'stone of stumbling and rock of offence' to those who reject Him."], questions:[
{q:"What was Isaiah told to write with a man's pen on a great roll?", options:["Maher-shalal-hash-baz","Immanuel","Shear-jashub","Woe to Ariel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:1",verse:"Moreover the LORD said unto me, Take thee a great roll, and write in it with a man’s pen concerning Maher-shalal-hash-baz.", insight:"The name meant 'swift to the spoil, hasty to the prey.' God advertised coming judgment in a child's name, so the nation would read the warning every time the boy was called."},
{q:"How was the message on the great roll to be written?", options:["With a man's pen","With a chisel of iron","With the finger of God","With a reed of brass"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:1",verse:"Moreover the LORD said unto me, Take thee a great roll, and write in it with a man’s pen concerning Maher-shalal-hash-baz.", insight:"Plain tools for plain warnings. God's message needed no ornament to be true, and ordinary handwriting carried an extraordinary word."},
{q:"Which two faithful witnesses did Isaiah take to record the prophecy?", options:["Uriah the priest and Zechariah the son of Jeberechiah","Ahaz and Hezekiah","Shear-jashub and Immanuel","Eliakim and Shebna"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:2",verse:"And I took unto me faithful witnesses to record, Uriah the priest, and Zechariah the son of Jeberechiah.", insight:"God confirms His word with witnesses. Truth never stands on one voice alone, and heaven keeps receipts for the promises men will later doubt."},
{q:"Who is called 'the prophetess' in this chapter?", options:["Isaiah's wife","Huldah the prophetess","Miriam the sister of Moses","Deborah the judge"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:3",verse:"And I went unto the prophetess; and she conceived, and bare a son. Then said the LORD to me, Call his name Maher-shalal-hash-baz.", insight:"The prophetess was Isaiah's own wife. The whole family became a living parable to the nation, with God writing His message in their household, not just on scrolls."},
{q:"Before the child could cry 'my father' and 'my mother,' what would happen?", options:["The riches of Damascus and the spoil of Samaria would be taken by Assyria","The temple would be rebuilt in glory","Judah would be carried to Babylon","The king of Egypt would invade the land"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:4",verse:"For before the child shall have knowledge to cry, My father, and my mother, the riches of Damascus and the spoil of Samaria shall be taken away before the king of Assyria.", insight:"God timed prophecy to a toddler's vocabulary. Judgment would arrive before the boy could talk, showing that heaven's calendar runs faster than human fear expects."},
{q:"What gentle waters did this people refuse?", options:["The waters of Shiloah that go softly","The waters of the Jordan","The pools of Siloam","The springs of En-gedi"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:6",verse:"Forasmuch as this people refuseth the waters of Shiloah that go softly, and rejoice in Rezin and Remaliah’s son;", insight:"They despised God's quiet provision and craved noisy power. Rejecting the gentle leads to facing the violent, because contempt for small mercies invites great judgments."},
{q:"What would God bring upon them instead of the soft waters?", options:["The waters of the river, strong and many, even the king of Assyria","A drought of forty years","The flood of Noah","The plagues of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:7",verse:"Now therefore, behold, the Lord bringeth up upon them the waters of the river, strong and many, even the king of Assyria, and all his glory: and he shall come up over all his channels, and go over all his banks:", insight:"Refuse the stream and you get the flood. God gave them the torrent they preferred over the trickle, letting their chosen power become their punishment."},
{q:"How far would the Assyrian flood reach into Judah?", options:["Even to the neck","Only to the ankles","To the gates of the temple","To the borders of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:8",verse:"And he shall pass through Judah; he shall overflow and go over, he shall reach even to the neck; and the stretching out of his wings shall fill the breadth of thy land, O Immanuel.", insight:"Up to the neck means nearly drowned but not quite. Judgment would press hard, yet a remnant would keep its head above water by God's mercy."},
{q:"As the flood spreads, to whom does the land still belong?", options:["O Immanuel","O house of David","O daughter of Zion","O holy city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:8",verse:"And he shall pass through Judah; he shall overflow and go over, he shall reach even to the neck; and the stretching out of his wings shall fill the breadth of thy land, O Immanuel.", insight:"Even in judgment the land is called Immanuel's. God's presence with His people outlasts their punishment, and the name given in chapter 7 becomes their anchor in chapter 8."},
{q:"What will happen to the peoples that band together against God's people?", options:["They shall be broken in pieces","They shall prevail for a time","They shall become allies","They shall be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:9",verse:"Associate yourselves, O ye people, and ye shall be broken in pieces; and give ear, all ye of far countries: gird yourselves, and ye shall be broken in pieces; gird yourselves, and ye shall be broken in pieces.", insight:"Every confederacy against God breaks, and the verse repeats it three times for emphasis. Human alliances are brittle before heaven's resolve."},
{q:"Why would the counsel of the nations come to nothing?", options:["For God is with us","For their armies were small","For the walls were strong","For the king was wise"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:10",verse:"Take counsel together, and it shall come to nought; speak the word, and it shall not stand: for God is with us.", insight:"'God is with us' is the Hebrew meaning of Immanuel. The name itself was Judah's defense, and no counsel can stand against the God who stands with His people."},
{q:"In what was Isaiah instructed not to walk?", options:["The way of this people","The courts of the king","The streets of the city","The paths of the merchants"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:11",verse:"For the LORD spake thus to me with a strong hand, and instructed me that I should not walk in the way of this people, saying,", insight:"God trains His servants to differ. Following the crowd is not an option for the called, and a strong hand from God steadies the lonely path."},
{q:"What were they told not to call a confederacy?", options:["What this people called a confederacy","What the priests decreed","What the scribes had written","What the merchants traded"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:12",verse:"Say ye not, A confederacy, to all them to whom this people shall say, A confederacy; neither fear ye their fear, nor be afraid.", insight:"Popular panic names everything a conspiracy. Faith refuses the crowd's vocabulary of fear and will not let rumor set the agenda."},
{q:"Whom should they sanctify, fear, and dread instead?", options:["The LORD of hosts himself","The king of Assyria","The armies of Egypt","The coming famine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:13",verse:"Sanctify the LORD of hosts himself; and let him be your fear, and let him be your dread.", insight:"We all fear something. Isaiah says to relocate fear from circumstances to God, because dread aimed at heaven steadies the heart that dread aimed at earth would shatter."},
{q:"What will the LORD be to those who trust Him?", options:["A sanctuary","A hiding place in Egypt","A wall of bronze","A chariot of fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:14",verse:"And he shall be for a sanctuary; but for a stone of stumbling and for a rock of offence to both the houses of Israel, for a gin and for a snare to the inhabitants of Jerusalem.", insight:"The same God is refuge to some and ruin to others. Our posture toward Him decides which He becomes to us, sanctuary or stumbling stone."},
{q:"What will He be to the houses of Israel that reject Him?", options:["A stone of stumbling and a rock of offence","A consuming fire from heaven","A closed and bolted door","A silent and distant judge"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:14",verse:"And he shall be for a sanctuary; but for a stone of stumbling and for a rock of offence to both the houses of Israel, for a gin and for a snare to the inhabitants of Jerusalem.", insight:"Christ Himself became this stone, and the New Testament quotes this verse about Jesus. The foundation God lays becomes the rock men trip over when they refuse to build on it."},
{q:"What was Isaiah to bind up and seal among his disciples?", options:["The testimony and the law","The doors of the temple","The treasures of the king","The scrolls of the chronicles"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:16",verse:"Bind up the testimony, seal the law among my disciples.", insight:"When the nation rejects the word, God preserves it in a faithful few. Disciples carry what crowds discard, and the sealed testimony outlives the generation that ignored it."},
{q:"What did Isaiah resolve to do while the LORD hid His face?", options:["Wait upon the LORD and look for Him","Flee to the mountains","Hide within the temple","Seek out a new king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:17",verse:"And I will wait upon the LORD, that hideth his face from the house of Jacob, and I will look for him.", insight:"Waiting is faith's hardest work. Isaiah looked for God precisely when God seemed absent, which is the purest form of trust, seeking the face that is hidden."},
{q:"What would Isaiah and the children God gave him be in Israel?", options:["For signs and for wonders","For priests and Levites","For judges and rulers","For scribes and teachers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:18",verse:"Behold, I and the children whom the LORD hath given me are for signs and for wonders in Israel from the LORD of hosts, which dwelleth in mount Zion.", insight:"The family itself became the sermon. God writes messages in lives, not just on scrolls, and Isaiah's household preached every day without saying a word."},
{q:"To what standard must all teaching be tested?", options:["The law and the testimony","The wisdom of the elders","The dreams of the prophets","The customs of the fathers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 8:20",verse:"To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them.", insight:"Every voice must match God's word. 'If they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them,' a test that still exposes darkness dressed as counsel."}
]},
{n:9, name:"Unto Us a Child Is Born", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 9:6",verse:"For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace."}, facts:["Did you know the 'great light' of Isaiah 9:2 dawned exactly where the darkness was deepest? Jesus grew up in Nazareth of Galilee and began His ministry in Zebulun and Naphtali, the very lands named here.","Did you know Handel set 'For unto us a child is born' to music in his Messiah? Millions have sung Isaiah 9:6 who never knew they were singing a prophecy about 2,700 years old.","Did you know four times in this chapter's second half comes the refrain 'his anger is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still'? Judgment kept knocking because the people kept refusing to turn."], questions:[
{q:"Which two lands were first lightly afflicted, then more grievously?", options:["Zebulun and Naphtali","Judah and Benjamin","Ephraim and Manasseh","Gad and Reuben"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:1",verse:"Nevertheless the dimness shall not be such as was in her vexation, when at the first he lightly afflicted the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, and afterward did more grievously afflict her by the way of the sea, beyond Jordan, in Galilee of the nations.", insight:"The first to fall would be the first to see light. Galilee, despised and invaded, became the stage of Christ's ministry, because God loves to dawn where darkness was deepest."},
{q:"What have the people who walked in darkness seen?", options:["A great light","A new king","A pillar of fire","A shining city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:2",verse:"The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined.", insight:"Light comes to walkers in darkness, not to those who claim they already see. Grace finds us in the dark, and the darker the night, the brighter the dawn."},
{q:"Upon whom has the light shined?", options:["They that dwell in the land of the shadow of death","They that keep the law perfectly","They that dwell in Jerusalem","They that offer sacrifices daily"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:2",verse:"The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined.", insight:"The shadow of death is the darkest address on earth. No place is too dark for God's light to reach, and no valley too deep for dawn to find."},
{q:"To what is their joy compared?", options:["The joy in harvest and when men divide the spoil","The joy of a wedding feast","The joy of a newborn child","The joy of a freed captive"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:3",verse:"Thou hast multiplied the nation, and not increased the joy: they joy before thee according to the joy in harvest, and as men rejoice when they divide the spoil.", insight:"Harvest and spoil are joys after long labor. God's deliverance turns toil into celebration, and the reapers' shout becomes the song of the redeemed."},
{q:"What three instruments of bondage has God broken?", options:["The yoke of his burden, the staff of his shoulder, the rod of his oppressor","The sword, the spear, and the shield","The wall, the gate, and the tower","The crown, the scepter, and the throne"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:4",verse:"For thou hast broken the yoke of his burden, and the staff of his shoulder, the rod of his oppressor, as in the day of Midian.", insight:"God breaks the tools of oppression, not just the bonds. He destroys the yoke, the staff, and the rod itself, so that slavery cannot simply be rebuilt."},
{q:"To what famous deliverance is this victory compared?", options:["The day of Midian","The day of Jericho","The day of the Red Sea","The day of Carmel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:4",verse:"For thou hast broken the yoke of his burden, and the staff of his shoulder, the rod of his oppressor, as in the day of Midian.", insight:"In Gideon's day God routed an army with 300 men and broken pitchers. He delights in saving by weakness, so that no one mistakes the victory for human strength."},
{q:"What will become of the warrior's garments rolled in blood?", options:["They shall be for burning and fuel of fire","They shall be washed white as snow","They shall be given to the poor","They shall be buried in the field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:5",verse:"For every battle of the warrior is with confused noise, and garments rolled in blood; but this shall be with burning and fuel of fire.", insight:"This victory ends war itself. The uniforms of violence become firewood, because the Prince of Peace does not merely win battles, He abolishes them."},
{q:"What is given unto us?", options:["A child is born, a son is given","A kingdom is restored","A temple is built","A law is written"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:6",verse:"For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.", insight:"Salvation arrives as a baby, not an army. God's greatest gift came wrapped in weakness, born in a stable before He ever sat on a throne."},
{q:"Where shall the government rest?", options:["Upon his shoulder","In the hands of the elders","Upon the throne of Rome","In the courts of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:6",verse:"For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.", insight:"The same shoulder that bore the yoke of the cross bears the government of the world. The place of His suffering became the seat of His rule."},
{q:"With what will He order and establish His kingdom?", options:["With judgment and with justice","With armies and chariots","With gold and silver","With signs and wonders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:7",verse:"Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even for ever. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will perform this.", insight:"His reign is founded on righteousness, not force. Justice is the architecture of His kingdom, and judgment its cornerstone, which is why it cannot fall."},
{q:"Of the increase of His government and peace, what is promised?", options:["There shall be no end","It shall last a thousand years","It shall fill the temple","It shall conquer Rome"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:7",verse:"Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even for ever. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will perform this.", insight:"Human empires peak and fall, but His kingdom only grows, forever. Every other government has an expiry date; His has an open horizon."},
{q:"In what attitude did Ephraim receive God's word?", options:["In pride and stoutness of heart","In humility and repentance","In fear and trembling","In silence and awe"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:9",verse:"And all the people shall know, even Ephraim and the inhabitant of Samaria, that say in the pride and stoutness of heart,", insight:"Pride hears judgment as information, not warning. A stout heart turns God's word into background noise, and that is exactly what makes the next blow certain."},
{q:"After the bricks fell down, what did proud Ephraim boast they would do?", options:["Build with hewn stones","Flee to Egypt for help","Repent in sackcloth and ashes","Rebuild with straw and clay"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:10",verse:"The bricks are fallen down, but we will build with hewn stones: the sycomores are cut down, but we will change them into cedars.", insight:"Pride rebuilds bigger after judgment instead of repenting. Self-confidence is the refusal to learn, and 'we will' after God's 'woe' is the language of a hard heart."},
{q:"Whom would the LORD set up against Ephraim?", options:["The adversaries of Rezin","The armies of Babylon","The Philistines alone","The king of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:11",verse:"Therefore the LORD shall set up the adversaries of Rezin against him, and join his enemies together;", insight:"God turns former allies into adversaries. The help Ephraim trusted became the hand that struck, a warning against leaning on any arm but God's."},
{q:"After all this judgment, what did the people still refuse to do?", options:["Turn unto Him that smote them","Offer sacrifices in the temple","Keep the Sabbath day","Pay tribute to the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:13",verse:"For the people turneth not unto him that smiteth them, neither do they seek the LORD of hosts.", insight:"The smiting was meant to turn them, but unrepentant hearts waste even God's discipline. Pain without repentance is just pain, and they chose the pain."},
{q:"According to verse 15, who is the 'tail' that God would cut off?", options:["The prophet that teacheth lies","The king of Samaria","The merchants of Tyre","The elders of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:15",verse:"The ancient and honourable, he is the head; and the prophet that teacheth lies, he is the tail.", insight:"False prophets are the tail because they follow and wag the nation's lies. Leaders who deceive drag everyone down with them, which is why truth in the pulpit matters so much."},
{q:"What did the leaders of this people cause them to do?", options:["To err","To prosper","To rebel openly","To flee the land"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:16",verse:"For the leaders of this people cause them to err; and they that are led of them are destroyed.", insight:"Leaders carry weight. When guides err, the guided are destroyed, so the stewardship of influence is one of the heaviest trusts God gives."},
{q:"Why would the Lord have no joy in their young men?", options:["For every one is an hypocrite and an evildoer","For they refused to fight","For they worshipped idols in secret","For they fled to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:17",verse:"Therefore the Lord shall have no joy in their young men, neither shall have mercy on their fatherless and widows: for every one is an hypocrite and an evildoer, and every mouth speaketh folly. For all this his anger is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still.", insight:"Hypocrisy empties even the young of promise. God takes no pleasure in strength without integrity, and talent without truth is a grief to heaven."},
{q:"To what is wickedness compared?", options:["Fire that burneth and devours briers and thorns","A flood that overflows its banks","A plague that spreads in the night","A sword that cannot be sheathed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:18",verse:"For wickedness burneth as the fire: it shall devour the briers and thorns, and shall kindle in the thickets of the forest, and they shall mount up like the lifting up of smoke.", insight:"Wickedness is self-consuming. The fire sinners kindle becomes the fire that burns them, and evil is its own executioner in the end."},
{q:"What chilling picture closes this chapter's judgment?", options:["They shall eat every man the flesh of his own arm","The sun shall be darkened at noon","The sea shall give up its dead","The mountains shall melt like wax"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 9:20",verse:"And he shall snatch on the right hand, and be hungry; and he shall eat on the left hand, and they shall not be satisfied: they shall eat every man the flesh of his own arm:", insight:"A nation that devours itself ends in chaos, each man feeding on his own. Sin turns neighbors into prey, and the final stage of selfishness is self-destruction."}
]},
{n:10, name:"The Axe and the Woodsman", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 10:15",verse:"Shall the axe boast itself against him that heweth therewith? or shall the saw magnify itself against him that shaketh it? as if the rod should shake itself against them that lift it up, or as if the staff should lift up itself, as if it were no wood."}, facts:["Did you know Assyria really did march the exact road Isaiah names in verses 28 to 32? Sennacherib's invasion of 701 BC came down through Aiath, Michmash, Geba, and Nob toward Jerusalem, town by town as foretold.","Did you know the 'axe' question of verse 15 became a classic picture of pride? A tool boasting against its user is as absurd as a nation boasting against the God who raised it up.","Did you know Isaiah's 'remnant' promise runs through the whole Bible? Paul quotes Isaiah 10:22 in Romans 9 to show that God always keeps a faithful remnant, even when the nation falls."], questions:[
{q:"Against whom is the 'woe' pronounced at the chapter's opening?", options:["Them that decree unrighteous decrees","The merchants of Tyre","The priests of the temple","The soldiers of Assyria"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:1",verse:"Woe unto them that decree unrighteous decrees, and that write grievousness which they have prescribed;", insight:"God judges lawmakers, not just lawbreakers. Unjust legislation is sin with a seal on it, and heaven holds a special woe for pens that write oppression."},
{q:"Who were made prey by the corrupt rulers?", options:["Widows and the fatherless","The priests and Levites","The merchants and traders","The soldiers and captains"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:2",verse:"To turn aside the needy from judgment, and to take away the right from the poor of my people, that widows may be their prey, and that they may rob the fatherless!", insight:"A society's righteousness is measured at its most vulnerable. Widows and orphans are heaven's audit, and how a nation treats them reveals its true accounts."},
{q:"What searching question does God ask the unjust rulers?", options:["What will ye do in the day of visitation?","Where is your treasure laid up?","Who shall stand in the battle?","When will ye turn and repent?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:3",verse:"And what will ye do in the day of visitation, and in the desolation which shall come from far? to whom will ye flee for help? and where will ye leave your glory?", insight:"Every abuse of power faces a day of accounting. The question is never whether it will come, but what the powerful will answer when glory is stripped away."},
{q:"What does God call Assyria?", options:["The rod of mine anger","The sword of my vengeance","The hammer of the nations","The scourge of the east"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:5",verse:"O Assyrian, the rod of mine anger, and the staff in their hand is mine indignation.", insight:"Empires are tools in God's hand. The mightiest army of the age was a stick He picked up and would lay down, which puts all human power in its place."},
{q:"Against whom would God send Assyria?", options:["An hypocritical nation, the people of my wrath","The righteous remnant","The faithful priests","The surrounding nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:6",verse:"I will send him against an hypocritical nation, and against the people of my wrath will I give him a charge, to take the spoil, and to take the prey, and to tread them down like the mire of the streets.", insight:"God can use an ungodly nation to discipline His own people. Chastisement sometimes wears an enemy's uniform, and the rod is chosen by heaven, not by merit."},
{q:"What was in Assyria's heart, unlike God's purpose?", options:["To destroy and cut off nations not a few","To serve the LORD of hosts","To show mercy to Judah","To send the captives home"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:7",verse:"Howbeit he meaneth not so, neither doth his heart think so; but it is in his heart to destroy and cut off nations not a few.", insight:"God overrules evil intentions. Assyria meant conquest, God meant correction, and God's meaning prevailed, which is how He writes straight with crooked instruments."},
{q:"What did the king of Assyria boast about his princes?", options:["They were altogether kings","They were mightier than angels","They were wiser than Daniel","They were richer than Solomon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:8",verse:"For he saith, Are not my princes altogether kings?", insight:"Pride counts its captains as kings. Arrogance always inflates the org chart, and boasting about subordinates is the first symptom of a swollen head."},
{q:"Which cities does Assyria name as already conquered?", options:["Calno, Carchemish, Hamath, and Arpad","Tyre, Sidon, Gaza, and Ashkelon","Nineveh, Babylon, Ur, and Haran","Memphis, Thebes, Zoan, and Noph"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:9",verse:"Is not Calno as Carchemish? is not Hamath as Arpad? is not Samaria as Damascus?", insight:"Past victories became pride's portfolio. Assyria read its rap sheet as a resume, forgetting that every conquest had been permitted by a higher hand."},
{q:"What did Assyria assume about Jerusalem?", options:["It would fall like Samaria fell","It was protected by Egypt","Its God was stronger than idols","It had no walls worth besieging"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:11",verse:"Shall I not, as I have done unto Samaria and her idols, so do to Jerusalem and her idols?", insight:"Assyria mistook Jerusalem's God for Samaria's idols. The gravest error in spiritual warfare is assuming the living God is just another god among gods."},
{q:"What would God do after finishing His work upon Jerusalem?", options:["Punish the stout heart of the king of Assyria","Crown the king of Judah","Rebuild the walls of Samaria","Bless the nations round about"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:12",verse:"Wherefore it shall come to pass, that when the Lord hath performed his whole work upon mount Zion and on Jerusalem, I will punish the fruit of the stout heart of the king of Assyria, and the glory of his high looks.", insight:"God finishes with His people, then turns to their oppressor. The rod gets judged after it is used, and no instrument of heaven is exempt from heaven's justice."},
{q:"To what did Assyria credit his victories?", options:["The strength of his own hand and his wisdom","The favor of the LORD","The counsel of his prophets","The weakness of his enemies"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:13",verse:"For he saith, By the strength of my hand I have done it, and by my wisdom; for I am prudent: and I have removed the bounds of the people, and have robbed their treasures, and I have put down the inhabitants like a valiant man:", insight:"Self-made pride forgets the Hand behind the hand. Every 'I did it' ignores who gave the strength, and heaven keeps a record of stolen credit."},
{q:"To what did Assyria compare his gathering of the earth's riches?", options:["Gathering eggs left in a nest","Reaping a ripe harvest","Drawing water from a well","Shearing wool from sheep"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:14",verse:"And my hand hath found as a nest the riches of the people: and as one gathereth eggs that are left, have I gathered all the earth; and there was none that moved the wing, or opened the mouth, or peeped.", insight:"The proud see nations as helpless nests. But eggs in a nest belong to Another, and the gatherer will answer to the Owner of the nest."},
{q:"What question exposes Assyria's pride?", options:["Shall the axe boast itself against him that heweth therewith?","Shall the clay instruct the potter?","Shall the servant rule the master?","Shall the branch sustain the root?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:15",verse:"Shall the axe boast itself against him that heweth therewith? or shall the saw magnify itself against him that shaketh it? as if the rod should shake itself against them that lift it up, or as if the staff should lift up itself, as if it were no wood.", insight:"The tool never outranks the craftsman. Boasting against God is an axe lecturing the woodsman, and pride always forgets whose hand is swinging."},
{q:"What would the Lord send among Assyria's fat ones?", options:["Leanness","A plague of locusts","A famine of bread","A sword from the south"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:16",verse:"Therefore shall the Lord, the Lord of hosts, send among his fat ones leanness; and under his glory he shall kindle a burning like the burning of a fire.", insight:"God thins what pride fattens. Leanness is His answer to swollen self-sufficiency, and He knows exactly how to humble a well-fed empire."},
{q:"How few would Assyria's mighty men become?", options:["So few that a child may write them","So few they could hide in a cave","So few they could fit in one ship","So few they could be counted on one hand"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:19",verse:"And the rest of the trees of his forest shall be few, that a child may write them.", insight:"The forest becomes a list a child can write. God reduces the innumerable to the countable, and the army that terrified nations fits on a schoolboy's tablet."},
{q:"After Assyria's fall, upon whom would the remnant stay?", options:["Upon the LORD, the Holy One of Israel","Upon the king of Egypt","Upon their own armies","Upon the rebuilt walls"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:20",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the remnant of Israel, and such as are escaped of the house of Jacob, shall no more again stay upon him that smote them; but shall stay upon the LORD, the Holy One of Israel, in truth.", insight:"True repentance changes dependencies. The remnant stops leaning on the arm that beat them and learns to stay upon the LORD in truth."},
{q:"To whom would the remnant return?", options:["Unto the mighty God","Unto the land of Egypt","Unto the cities of the Philistines","Unto the mountains of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:21",verse:"The remnant shall return, even the remnant of Jacob, unto the mighty God.", insight:"Return is the gospel in one word. The remnant's long journey ends at the mighty God, which is where every wandering heart is meant to arrive."},
{q:"Why would the yoke be destroyed?", options:["Because of the anointing","Because of the army's strength","Because of the king's wisdom","Because of Egypt's help"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:27",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that his burden shall be taken away from off thy shoulder, and his yoke from off thy neck, and the yoke shall be destroyed because of the anointing.", insight:"Anointing breaks what armies cannot. God's Spirit, not human effort, snaps the yoke, and the burden lifts where heaven's oil has touched."},
{q:"Where did the Assyrian army lay up its carriages on the march toward Jerusalem?", options:["At Michmash","At Jericho","At Bethlehem","At Hebron"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:28",verse:"He is come to Aiath, he is passed to Migron; at Michmash he hath laid up his carriages:", insight:"The prophecy traces the invasion road town by town. God's foreknowledge names streets, not just nations, and history walked the exact route He mapped."},
{q:"What will happen to the high ones of stature and the haughty?", options:["They shall be hewn down and humbled","They shall be exalted forever","They shall flee to the islands","They shall make a lasting peace"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 10:33",verse:"Behold, the Lord, the LORD of hosts, shall lop the bough with terror: and the high ones of stature shall be hewn down, and the haughty shall be humbled.", insight:"God lops the tall trees. Humbling the haughty is His settled policy, and no height of pride is beyond the reach of His axe."}
]}
]);
